WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.670
When Steve Jobs died in 2011, his admirers
paid tribute around the world.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.050
This kind of turnout was usually reserved
for rock stars, not a CEO.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.330
Yet this age arguably belongs to those who
build the future.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:19.080
According to the Forbes list of the richest
people in the world in 2021, 7 out of the

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.810
top 10 made their fortune in engineering.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.340
Amazon’s Jeff Bezos.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.638
Tesla and SpaceX’s Elon Musk.

00:00:25.638 --> 00:00:27.168
Microsoft’s Bill Gates.

00:00:27.168 --> 00:00:28.697
Facebook's Mark Zuckerberg.

00:00:28.697 --> 00:00:30.400
Oracle’s Larry Ellison.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.739
Google’s Larry Page and Sergey Brin.

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:36.559
It’s hard to think of an engineer from the
seventies who was super well-known.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:41.930
Yet today’s engineers are household names
whether we love’em or love to hate’em.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.190
Musk even guest-hosted Saturday Night Live
which is usually reserved for famous actors

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.190
and artists.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.150
He’s a new kind of celebrity with such a
huge Twitter following that he’s a walking

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.960
advertisement for Tesla.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.309
In fact, Tesla doesn’t spend money on advertising
which I talked about in another video.

00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:00.070
So, how did engineers become cool?

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.839
They used to be known for building public
infrastructure like bridges and dams.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:08.900
Necessary for any functioning society but
not something that would normally attract

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:09.900
crowds.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.770
Then, Jobs came along.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.430
He didn’t have a technical background but he
had his hands in everything at Apple including

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.430
engineering.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.700
The world changed when he put the internet
in everyone’s pocket with the introduction

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.229
of the iPhone in 2007.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:28.590
The late Apple CEO built a following by creating
personal inventions that excited people.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.800
Since then, engineers have continued to create
products that are in your personal space - be

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.106
it a smartwatch or social media - which has
in turn made them famous.

00:01:37.106 --> 00:01:40.305
And the appetite for such products is only
growing.

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:44.740
Engineers are expected to experience a 3 percent
job growth in a decade which is on par with

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:50.366
average while the demand for software developers
is predicted to soar 22%.

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:54.939
They’re the ones responsible for writing
and designing code to develop apps.

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:58.000
In other words, creating the products we crave.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.729
An increasing number of people are choosing
the profession.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:05.689
In India, 30% of students pursued a degree
in STEM: science, technology, engineering,

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:11.099
and mathematics which produced almost 2.7
million graduates in 2018.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:16.270
Where I live, in Canada, enrollment in math,
computer, and information sciences has increased

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.740
47% comparing a recent academic year to an
earlier one.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.250
In a previous video, I mentioned that engineering
degrees are now more common than MBA degrees

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.780
amongst the world’s top-performing CEOs
which makes sense given some of the biggest

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.590
companies are tech giants.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.420
There’s also the belief that engineers rise
to the top because they’re good problem

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.420
solvers.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.290
Google’s former chief financial officer
Patrick Pichette once pointed out that “Google

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.480
is really an engineering company, with all
these computer scientists that see the world

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.910
as a completely broken place.”

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.050
Engineers try to solve the most pressing issues
of the day using technology.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.220
Musk is a self-taught engineer who wants to
reduce pollution with his electric vehicles

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.420
and is confident the century-long dominance
of the internal combustion engine - otherwise

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.020
known as “ICE” is coming to a close.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.000
Well, I don’t think there will be an ICE industry
long-term.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.503
There are still, like, some steam engines made somewhere but, like, they're just, 

00:03:08.503 --> 00:03:11.146
basically, sort of,  quirky collector's items. 

00:03:11.146 --> 00:03:13.180
That will be the future of the internal combustion
engine (ICE) car.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.239
At the same time, people like him have warned
of a dark side to technology.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:21.400
He believes super-intelligent machines could
pose a threat to humanity and has called for

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.750
government oversight of artificial intelligence, fearing
AI will surpass humans in intelligence in

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.260
the way we outsmart cats.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:32.349
Smart machines are expected to eliminate many
human jobs.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.709
FedEx has now teamed up with a robotics company
to test out autonomous delivery vehicles.

00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:41.380
Amazon runs cashier-less grocery stores and
wants to deliver packages by drone in under

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.840
30 minutes.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.720
The World Economic Forum says the rise of
automation will result in a loss of 85 million

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:54.659
jobs by 2025 yet in the same breath adds
97 million new jobs will be created such as

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:57.549
AI and machine learning specialists and data
analysts.

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:02.290
There’s also the argument that future technology
will redefine the meaning of the traditional

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.290
workweek.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.170
More than 100 years ago, people worked
70 hours a week on average.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.810
With the industrial revolution and labor laws,
it’s now down to about 35 hours.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:15.840
The former head of British Petroleum, Lord
John Browne, remarked: “Why do we then define

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.250
work as a 35-hour week?

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.550
Why can’t it be 17-and-a-half?”

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:25.270
Behind these forces of societal change are
engineers who dream big.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:30.020
Bezos’ space venture Blue Origin has dreamt
up space colonies straight out of seventies

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.060
science fiction.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:35.090
Though the plan hasn’t won over a fellow
billionaire who has a different vision of

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.830
humanity in space.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:42.000
Musk’s SpaceX expects Mars to be colonized
by a constant flow of humans traveling aboard

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.150
Starships.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:47.590
So long as we have a desire for new adventures
and new things, engineers will continue to

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.770
build the future and enjoy all the attention
that comes along with it.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.300
At the core of an engineer is exceptional
skill and ability.

